# Short, original paraphrases of well-known Bhagavad Gita teachings, keyed by chapter.verse.
# These are not verbatim quotations from any particular translator - they are brief restatements
# of the widely-known teaching of each verse, safe to display freely.

QUOTES = [
    ("2.47", "You have a right to your work, never to the fruits of it. Do your duty without being attached to the result."),
    ("2.20", "The soul is never born and never dies. It is eternal, ageless, and beyond destruction."),
    ("2.14", "Pleasure and pain, heat and cold, come and go like the seasons. Learn to bear them calmly."),
    ("2.48", "Perform your duty with an even mind, letting go of attachment to success or failure. This balance itself is yoga."),
    ("3.19", "Without attachment, always do the work that must be done, for one who works without attachment reaches the highest."),
    ("4.7", "Whenever goodness grows weak and evil grows strong, I take birth again to restore the balance."),
    ("4.8", "To protect the good, destroy the wicked, and re-establish righteousness, I come into being age after age."),
    ("6.5", "Lift yourself by your own efforts; do not let yourself sink. The mind alone is your friend, and also your enemy."),
    ("6.6", "For one who has mastered the mind, the mind is the best of friends; for one who has not, the mind works like an enemy."),
    ("18.66", "Let go of every other refuge and surrender to me alone. I will free you from every sorrow; do not fear."),
    ("2.62", "Dwelling on objects of desire, attachment to them arises; from attachment comes craving, and from craving, anger."),
    ("2.7", "When the mind is confused about duty, seek guidance humbly from one who sees the truth clearly."),
    ("6.35", "The mind is restless and hard to control, but it can be tamed through steady practice and detachment."),
    ("3.35", "It is better to do your own duty imperfectly than to do another's duty perfectly."),
    ("9.22", "For those who are ever devoted and think of nothing else, I carry what they lack and preserve what they have."),
    ("2.13", "Just as the body passes through childhood, youth and old age, the soul passes into another body. The wise are not troubled by this."),
    ("15.7", "The living being in this world is an eternal fragment of the divine, struggling with the mind and senses of nature."),
    ("12.15", "One who does not disturb the world and is not disturbed by it, who is free from anxiety and fear, is dear to me."),
    ("16.3", "Purity, self-control, simplicity, and freedom from malice mark one born for a higher, gentler nature."),
    ("2.71", "One who has given up every desire, who moves without craving, without the sense of 'I' and 'mine', attains peace."),
]